WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:06.260
Secure serverless Node.js operation starts with inventory.

2
00:00:06.360 --> 00:00:22.600
List functions, triggers, execution identities, permissions, secrets, dependencies, deployment packages, downstream services, queues, topics, object stores, databases, logs, metrics, and owners.

3
00:00:22.700 --> 00:00:33.260
Map event flows and trust boundaries so reviewers can see who can trigger each function, what data arrives, what authority the function has, and what happens on failure.

4
00:00:33.360 --> 00:00:38.880
Without that map, teams tend to review isolated handler code and miss workflow-level risk.

5
00:00:39.160 --> 00:00:43.480
Keep packages minimal, repeatable, and free of secrets.

6
00:00:43.580 --> 00:00:46.060
Use lockfiles and dependency review.

7
00:00:46.160 --> 00:00:51.200
Keep runtimes, layers, adapters, and shared packages current.

8
00:00:51.300 --> 00:00:56.980
Apply least privilege per function or workflow instead of reusing broad roles.

9
00:00:57.080 --> 00:01:07.640
Validate every event at the handler boundary, enforce authentication and authorization, and protect object ownership and tenant boundaries server-side.

10
00:01:07.740 --> 00:01:15.180
Treat managed services as helpful transport and execution platforms, not as substitutes for application security.

11
00:01:15.460 --> 00:01:19.540
Protect secrets through approved mechanisms and safe application behavior.

12
00:01:19.640 --> 00:01:29.480
Do not log full process.env, credentials, tokens, private URLs, authorization headers, cookies, or full sensitive payloads.

13
00:01:29.580 --> 00:01:39.900
Control database connections, outbound calls, storage access, timeouts, memory, concurrency, retry behavior, and idempotency deliberately.

14
00:01:40.000 --> 00:01:49.760
Use dead-letter handling, alerts, logs, metrics, traces, and runbooks so failures are visible and recoverable instead of quietly expensive.

15
00:01:50.040 --> 00:02:11.080
A practical first-week plan is direct: inventory functions and triggers, review execution roles, find broad permissions, check secret handling, validate event schemas, add idempotency to risky async handlers, confirm timeout and concurrency settings, redact logs, and review infrastructure-as-code changes.

16
00:02:11.180 --> 00:02:17.100
Then make those checks part of pull requests, CI/CD, monitoring, and incident response.

17
00:02:17.200 --> 00:02:23.440
Serverless reduces infrastructure chores, but secure operation still requires clear engineering ownership.
